  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "wCgHUMaPFfcYak3mtsHk01+FNQs=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:55:16.184Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "\"Packages of documents are a core structuring mechanism in the Vetra framework, allowing developers to group and manage related documents efficiently. These packages serve as modular collections of **document specifications and modules**, ensuring consistency, scalability, and reusability across different applications.\"\n\nThe example groupings are all **domain-shaped rather than technical**, which is the point worth taking:\n\n- **Finance** \u2014 invoices, payments, budgets, financial reporting;\n- **Contributor Billing** \u2014 tracking work, invoicing, and paying contributors in fiat and crypto;\n- **Governance** \u2014 proposals, voting, contributor agreements, decision-making;\n- **People Ops** \u2014 contributor profiles, roles, task assignments, reputation;\n- **Project Management** \u2014 task tracking, milestones, resource allocation, deliverables.\n\nA package is therefore the unit at which a *domain* is shipped, not the unit at which code is organised \u2014 which is consistent with how a package is loaded at runtime and how Connect can install one on encountering an unknown document type.\n\nThe **package library at `vetra.io/packages`** is the discovery surface: it lists packages built by different builder teams, and each entry links to the **GitHub repository with the package's source code** \u2014 so a package is browsable as a worked example, not just installable.\n\n## Provenance\n- `apps/academy/docs/academy/03-Build/06-ExampleUsecases/VetraPackageLibrary/VetraPackageLibrary.md` \u2014 whole page: definition, example packages, the package library link\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:55:16.184Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:55:16.184Z"
    }
  },
